@meta title="AGD in five minutes" audience=developers difficulty=beginner

@h1 AGD in five minutes [#root]
@p AGD is a line-oriented document format. Every block starts at column 0 with
@p `@<tag>`. That is the whole syntactic story.

@h2 Blocks [#blocks]
@p Blocks are independent units. Use `@h1`-`@h4` for headings and `@p` for
@p paragraphs.

@code lang=agd
~~~
@h1 Hello world
@p This is a paragraph.
~~~

@h2 Stable IDs [#stable-ids]
@p Add `[#name]` at the end of any block to make it addressable.

@code lang=agd
~~~
@h1 Welcome [#intro]
@p Body of the intro [#intro-body]
~~~

@h2 Lists and quotes [#lists]
@p Lists use `@ul` or `@ol`. Each item is a `- ` line. Lists end at the next
@p empty line or the next `@<tag>`.

@code lang=agd
~~~
@ul
- one
- two
- three
~~~

@h2 Code blocks [#code]
@p Use `@code` plus a triple-tilde fence for verbatim content. The `lang=`
@p attribute tags the language for downstream tooling.

@code lang=agd
~~~
@code lang=python
~~~~
def hello():
    return "world"
~~~~
~~~

@h2 References [#refs]
@p Use `@ref #id` to point to another block. The CLI verifies refs.

@code lang=shell
~~~
agd ref --check my-doc.agd
~~~

@h2 Editing safely [#editing]
@p Agents edit through stable IDs, not text positions. The `agd edit` command
@p applies a JSON operation to a single block.

@code lang=shell
~~~
agd edit doc.agd --op '{"op":"replace","id":"intro","with":{"kind":"p","content":{"type":"inline","value":[{"kind":"text","text":"new body"}]}}}'
~~~

@h2 Where to next [#next]
@ul
- read the spec — `spec/AGD-SPEC.agd`
- run the token benchmark — `agd bench examples/api-doc.agd`
- try converting from Markdown — `agd convert from-md README.md`
